cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  Any publication based upon results obtained with ATOMCI2 must include the
  attribution "This work is based, in part, on results from the ATOMCI
  package." and refer to the two works on ATOMCI listed under references
  (LEGENDS.txt, Section 4). Results of the C tools should also cite the
  ATOMCI2 technical note.
title: "ATOMCI2: ATOMCI with C tools for spin-dependent atomic structure (Breit-Pauli terms and properties)"
version: 1.0.0
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Sasaki
    given-names: Fukashi
  - family-names: Sekiya
    given-names: Masahiro
  - family-names: Noro
    given-names: Takeshi
  - family-names: Ohtsuki
    given-names: Kazumasa
  - family-names: Osanai
    given-names: You
  - family-names: Miura
    given-names: Nobuaki
  - family-names: Honda
    given-names: Hiroaki
repository-code: "https://github.com/emol-project/atomci2"
license-url: "https://github.com/emol-project/atomci2/blob/main/LEGENDS.txt"
abstract: >-
  ATOMCI2 is the ATOMCI package with C tools for spin-dependent atomic
  structure. The Fortran program ATOMCI computes non-relativistic
  configuration interaction wave functions and energies of many-electron
  atoms in LS coupling. The C tools acatsp and acfs add an
  intermediate-coupling CI with the Breit-Pauli terms and the properties
  g_J, hyperfine A and B, transition rates E1 to M3 and isotope-shift
  parameters; the library hexpr-atomci is their angular engine. The
  Python code in heff/ builds effective Hamiltonians of 3d^N ions from
  ATOMCI CI matrices and projects them onto the operators of the
  parametric theory. Non-commercial research and educational use only.
keywords:
  - configuration interaction
  - atomic structure
  - LS coupling
  - tensor operators
  - coefficients of fractional parentage
  - MCSCF
  - Breit-Pauli
  - spin-orbit
  - Lande g factor
  - hyperfine structure
  - transition probabilities
  - isotope shift
  - effective Hamiltonian
  - three-body operators
  - transition-metal ions
